Standard Input
===============================================================================

The CLI standard-input context exposes one read-only Unicode text snapshot.

```mech
+> @in := cli/stdin
text := @in/text
```

1. Snapshot semantics
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The only admitted path is text. On the first runtime read, the standard backend
reads stdin through EOF and returns its exact UTF-8 text, including empty input,
line endings and combining characters. Invalid UTF-8 and I/O failures are errors;
there is no lossy conversion or numeric parsing.

Each provider retains its first result, including an error. Later reads reuse
that snapshot and do not consume stdin again. A new provider starts a new
snapshot. Reads return the complete stream rather than one line at a time.

2. Planning and capabilities
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Planning checks the context and exact text path and supplies a string placeholder.
It never reads stdin or waits for EOF. No input driver is required. Assignments
and sends to stdin are rejected.

Binding +> @in := cli/stdin grants no authority. Standard-input reads are excluded
from default CLI capabilities. Explicitly grant the existing read capability:

```mech
config := {
  run: {
    grants: [{ target: "cli/stdin" operations: ["read"] paths: ["text"] }]
  }
}
```

Configured CLI aliases use their instance target, such as term/stdin. The
experimental native Sequence compiler lowers the default CLI context to a
SnapshotTextRead observation. It retains the exact canonical cli://cli/stdin,
stdin context, text path and read intent in its resource requirements; its
launcher chooses the existing secure or trusted host policy.

3. Embedding
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Custom CLI backends implement stdin_text() to supply a text snapshot. Existing
backends can omit this optional method; reads then report stdin unavailable.
The provider caches the first success or failure for custom backends as well.
